{"question_id": "8a2466db", "hypothesis": "Step 1 \u2014 extract relevant information from your history\n- You already use Adobe Premiere Pro and asked about advanced settings, Lumetri Color Panel, Color Match, Creative panel, Curves (including Toe/Shoulder) and Color Wheels \u2014 so you\u2019re working beyond beginner level and are focused on color grading and advanced Premiere workflows.\n- You edit video (multi-camera, proxies, audio, export) were topics in earlier conversation about advanced Premiere features.\n- You value practical, tutorial-style learning (asked about where to start and specific features).\n- You also shoot photography and use cameras (Nikon D750, Rolleiflex, etc.), so you likely have footage you can use for practice and care about image quality and color.\n\nStep 2 \u2014 reasoned recommendations (what to learn next and where)\nGiven your existing Premiere Pro interest and specific questions about Lumetri/Curves, a good approach is:\n1. Learn firm foundations (Premiere interface, timeline, basic editing, export).\n2. Move to intermediate workflows (proxies, multicam, audio mixing, performance/encode).\n3. Focus on color grading (Lumetri basics \u2192 Creative look \u2192 Curves, Color Wheels, scopes \u2192 reference-driven matching).\n4. Practice integration (After Effects Dynamic Link, Media Encoder, LUTs, plugins).\n5. Study editing theory and storytelling to make your edits stronger creatively (not just technically).\n\nCurated resources (organized by type)\n\nOfficial / free documentation and starter projects\n- Adobe Premiere Pro Learn & Support (official tutorials and step-by-step projects) \u2014 start here for up-to-date feature walk-throughs and sample projects.\n- Adobe Help pages for Lumetri Color (detailed documentation of panels, scopes, Color Match).\n\nYouTube channels (free, practical, project-based)\n- Premiere Gal \u2014 practical Premiere workflows, effects, plugins, and templates.\n- Cinecom.net \u2014 creative effects, Lumetri looks, speed editing tutorials.\n- Justin Odisho \u2014 clean, practical Premiere & Lumetri techniques.\n- Premiere Bro \u2014 optimization, export settings, advanced tips.\n- Film Riot and RocketJump Film School \u2014 practical filmmaking/editing techniques that help storytelling.\n- Juan Melara (color grading focused) and Casey Faris / Alex Jordan (for Resolve & color concepts transferable to Lumetri).\n\nStructured (paid and subscription) courses\n- LinkedIn Learning (Premiere Pro courses): good structured pathway from basics to advanced (search \u201cPremiere Pro: Advanced\u201d and \u201cColor Grading\u201d).\n- Udemy: many highly-rated Premiere courses (look for recent, highly reviewed instructors like Phil Ebiner or others).\n- Skillshare: project-based short classes \u2014 good for creative looks and specific workflows.\n- Masterclass / specialized color grading sites: Color Grading Central (paid) and Mixing Light (paid) for deeper color grading theory and practice.\n\nBooks and editing theory (to improve storytelling/creative skill)\n- In the Blink of an Eye \u2014 Walter Murch (editing theory and rhythm).\n- The Technique of Film Editing \u2014 Karel Reisz & Gavin Millar or Ken Dancyger (practical and theoretical foundations).\n- The Visual Story \u2014 Bruce Block (composition, visual structure, very useful for editors/directors).\n\nColor-grading specific resources\n- Tutorials and articles on Color Match, Curves, Color Wheels (Adobe docs + YouTube channels above).\n- Juan Melara\u2019s grading tutorials (filmic curves and LUTs concepts) \u2014 good for toe/shoulder and film looks.\n- Consider installing DaVinci Resolve (free) to learn color grading workflows and scopes; many color concepts carry back to Lumetri.\n\nCommunities, forums, and feedback\n- Reddit: r/premiere and r/filmmakers for questions, workflow tips, presets.\n- Adobe Premiere Pro Community / Adobe Forums \u2014 for bug/workflow solutions.\n- Creative COW forum and Stack Exchange Video Production \u2014 technical help and best practices.\n\nPractice assets and plug-ins\n- Free stock footage for practice: Mixkit, Pexels Video, Pixabay.\n- Free LUTs and presets to study: Motion Array, Cinegrade, Color Grading Central (study how looks are built).\n- Plugins and templates: Motion Array, Envato Elements (practice with project templates).\n\nSuggested learning path / practical exercises\n1. Run a short official Premiere beginner \u2192 intermediate tutorial series to refresh UI and timeline shortcuts.\n2. Do a small project: edit a 1\u20132 minute short from raw footage. Export different presets to learn codecs.\n3. Regrade that short using Lumetri: start with a neutral baseline, apply a Creative Look, then refine with Curves and Color Wheels. Use scopes (Waveform, Vectorscope) to check skin tones and levels.\n4. Try Color Match across two different clips/cameras; then manually tweak Curves/Toe/Shoulder to understand what the auto-match changed.\n5. Learn a specific advanced workflow: create proxies for 4K workflow, and create a multicam edit.\n6. Get feedback in a community thread and iterate.\n\nExtras you might like\n- Podcasts and blogs about editing & post-production: The Art of the Cut (podcast), No Film School (articles/tutorials), ProVideo Coalition.\n\nIf you\u2019d like, I can:\n- Suggest a concrete 4\u20136 week curriculum tailored to how many hours per week you can practice.\n- Recommend 3-5 specific YouTube tutorials (by URL/title) for Lumetri Curves, Color Match, and export settings.\n- Point you to free sample projects you can download and practice with.\n\nWhich of those would you prefer next?", "autoeval_label": {"model": "gpt-4o-2024-08-06", "label": true}}
{"question_id": "75832dbd", "hypothesis": "Step 1 \u2014 Extract relevant information from our past chats\n- You work in deep learning for medical image analysis and asked previously about: segmentation datasets (BRATS, MSD, etc.), multimodal fusion, transfer learning, domain adaptation, 3D/volumetric analysis, transformers, federated learning, and explainable/interpretable AI (Grad-CAM, attention, saliency, LIME/SHAP).\n- You\u2019re also active in sustainability / \u201cGo Green\u201d and sustainable fashion (planning Twitter chats, guests like Maxine B\u00e9dat, social media campaigning).\n- You\u2019re interested in inclusion/DEI and LGBTQ+ issues (pronouns, intersectional feminism, workplace inclusion).\n- You like meeting people through book clubs and community events.\n\nStep 2 \u2014 Reasoning\nYou\u2019ll probably find the most value from (a) top conferences and workshops that publish the latest methods in medical imaging, explainability, domain adaptation and federated learning, and (b) practitioner/industry events and journals that cover sustainable fashion and inclusion. Below I list recent/high-impact venues and publications organized by those interests, plus ways to keep current.\n\nRecommendations\n\nA. Deep learning for medical imaging, segmentation, domain adaptation, XAI, federated learning\nConferences / workshops (look for proceedings and workshop papers):\n- MICCAI (Medical Image Computing & Computer Assisted Intervention) \u2014 flagship for medical imaging methods, segmentation, multi-modal fusion, transformers for segmentation.\n- MIDL (Medical Imaging with Deep Learning) \u2014 focused specifically on deep learning for medical imaging; good for state-of-the-art methods and code.\n- ISBI (IEEE Int. Symposium on Biomedical Imaging) \u2014 strong on algorithmic and applied imaging work.\n- NeurIPS / ICLR / ICML \u2014 main ML venues; check workshops on interpretability, fairness, federated learning, and medical imaging.\n- CVPR / ECCV \u2014 vision venues with medical imaging workshops (also good for new architectures like vision transformers).\n- RSNA / Radiological Society meetings \u2014 clinically focused, good for translational work and clinical validation.\n\nJournals and collections:\n- Medical Image Analysis (MedIA) \u2014 top journal for important algorithmic and validation work.\n- IEEE Transactions on Medical Imaging (TMI)\n- Radiology: Artificial Intelligence\n- Nature Machine Intelligence and Nature Biomedical Engineering \u2014 high-impact interdisciplinary papers\n- IEEE Journal of Biomedical and Health Informatics (JBHI)\n\nWhere to find recent papers:\n- arXiv (search / set alerts for keywords: \u201cmedical image segmentation\u201d, \u201cexplainable AI medical imaging\u201d, \u201cdomain adaptation medical\u201d)\n- Papers With Code (track tasks/datasets like BRATS, MSD)\n- Proceedings / workshop pages of the conferences above\n\nB. Explainable AI (XAI) \u2014 interpretability & visualization in medical imaging\nConferences/workshops:\n- NeurIPS / ICLR / ICML interpretability workshops\n- FAT* / AIES (AI Ethics & Society) for fairness and evaluation of explanations\n- MICCAI workshops on interpretability/clinical integration\n\nJournals & surveys:\n- Look for recent surveys and special issues in MedIA, Nature Machine Intelligence, and IEEE TMI on XAI in medicine.\n- Key technique papers to revisit (for background and citations): Grad-CAM, DeepLIFT, SHAP (these are foundational and often cited in newer applied XAI work).\n\nC. Sustainability / sustainable fashion (practice + research)\nConferences & industry events:\n- Copenhagen Fashion Summit \u2014 major industry summit on sustainability in fashion.\n- Sustainable Brands \u2014 cross-industry sustainability conference with sessions on fashion.\n- Textile Exchange (annual conferences and reports) \u2014 industry data and best practices.\n- Fashion Revolution events and Fashion Revolution Week (good for activism and community engagement