t-4o-2024-08-06", "label": true}}
{"question_id": "caf03d32", "hypothesis": "Step 1 \u2014 extract relevant facts from your history\n- You have a working slow cooker and have successfully made beef stew, but are \u201cstruggling\u201d overall with slow cooker recipes.\n- You\u2019ve asked about many slow-cooker topics: general recipes, vegetarian/vegan slow-cooker dishes (lentil curry, black bean chili, roasted veg stew, chickpea curry, vegan mac & cheese, etc.), and slow-cooker yogurt techniques.\n- For yogurt you\u2019ve asked specifically about:\n  - Whether a thermometer is necessary (you were told it\u2019s highly recommended).\n  - The classic dairy slow\u2011cooker method: heat milk to ~180\u2013185\u00b0F, cool to ~110\u2013120\u00b0F, add starter, incubate 8\u201312 hours; wrap slow cooker to insulate.\n  - Cashew-based yogurt: soaking cashews 4+ hours, blending (rough 1:1 cashew:water starting point), straining options (cheesecloth, nut\u2011milk bag, fine mesh), and using vegan starters/probiotics or thickeners.\n- You want better, consistent results (general improvement across recipes + specifics for vegan/cashew yogurt).\n\nStep 2 \u2014 reasoned, step\u2011by\u2011step advice to get better slow\u2011cooker results\n\nA. General slow\u2011cooker principles (apply to all recipes)\n1. Read the recipe and prep everything first (mise en place). Slow cooking is \u201cset-and-forget\u201d only if prepped.\n2. Browning = flavor. Sear meats and saut\u00e9 aromatics (onion, garlic, spices) on the stovetop before adding to the slow cooker. Deglaze the pan and add those juices to the slow cooker for extra depth.\n3. Layer smartly:\n   - Dense root vegetables (potatoes, carrots) benefit from being placed on the bottom or cut smaller because they need more heat/time.\n   - Tender vegetables (zucchini, bell pepper) should be added later or cut larger to avoid turning to mush.\n4. Liquid: slow cookers don\u2019t lose much moisture. Use about 3/4 of the liquid a stovetop recipe calls for (follow recipe guidance). If the end result is too thin, thicken at the end (see #9).\n5. Timing: low = longer (usually 7\u20139 hr), high = shorter (3\u20135 hr). Don\u2019t move the lid\u2014each lift drops temp ~15\u201320\u00b0F and slows cooking.\n6. Seasoning: add coarse aromatics and spices early, but salt and delicate fresh herbs finish better at the end. Taste and adjust salt/acidity at the finish.\n7. Dairy: add milk, cream, cheese, or yogurt near the end to avoid curdling or breaking.\n8. Check doneness early the first few times you try a recipe so you learn your cooker\u2019s quirks (some run hotter than others).\n9. Thickening & finishing:\n   - Remove lid for the last 30\u201360 minutes to reduce liquid.\n   - Use a slurry (cornstarch or arrowroot mixed with cold water) or whisk in cream/coconut cream to thicken.\n   - For long-braised sauces, finish by flashing under stovetop heat to reduce.\n10. Use fattier or tougher cuts for long cook times (chuck roast, pork shoulder); lean cuts can dry out\u2014add them later or braise in more liquid.\n\nB. Vegetarian / vegan slow\u2011cooker tips\n1. Beans: either pre-soak beans OR use canned beans. If using dried kidney beans, parboil 10 minutes on stove before adding (kidney beans contain a toxin if undercooked).\n2. Textures: plant proteins (tofu, tempeh, seitan) and quick-cooking veges are best added late to avoid mushiness.\n3. Umami boost: add miso (stirred in off heat), soy/tamari, nutritional yeast, tomato paste, or mushrooms for depth.\n4. Thickening: starchy veg (potatoes, butternut) will thicken stews; for creamy vegan results, add blended white beans, cashew cream, coconut cream, or a starch slurry near the end.\n5. Spices: bloom whole spices in oil on stovetop first when possible (improves fragrance and flavor).\n\nC. Troubleshooting common issues\n1. Result is bland: add salt and an acid (lemon, vinegar) at the end; finish with fresh herbs or a splash of soy/tamari for umami.\n2. Vegetables are mushy: cut larger, add later, or reduce cook time/temperature.\n3. Meat is dry or stringy: cook less time or use a fattier, tougher cut which will become tender instead of dry.\n4. Too much liquid: remove lid for final hour(s) or thicken with slurry/cornstarch.\n5. Scorched bottom: reduce heat, add more liquid, stir occasionally, or use a slow cooker liner and avoid high setting for long periods.\n\nD. Slow\u2011cooker yogurt (dairy) \u2014 practical method & pitfalls\n1. Use a thermometer \u2014 it\u2019s the most reliable way to get consistent yogurt.\n2. Steps:\n   - Pour milk into slow cooker; heat to 180\u2013185\u00b0F (82\u201385\u00b0C) to denature proteins and kill unwanted bacteria (2\u20133 hours on low typically).\n   - Turn off and cool to 110\u2013120\u00b0F (43\u201349\u00b0C).\n   - Stir in starter: 2 tablespoons plain yogurt (with live cultures) per quart milk, or use a commercial yogurt starter.\n   - Keep warm and insulated: wrap the cooker in towels/blankets or use the \u201cwarm\u201d setting if it keeps temps steady near 110\u2013115\u00b0F. Incubate 8\u201312 hours (longer = tangier and thicker).\n   - Chill thoroughly to set and stop fermentation.\n3. Problems & fixes:\n   - Won\u2019t set: culture killed (starter added too hot), temp was too low, or insufficient incubation time. Use fresh starter and monitor temps.\n   - Too sour: incubated too long or too warm\u2014shorten time next batch.\n   - Grainy/curdled: overheating or overheating starter. Cool to correct temp before adding cultures.\n\nE. Cashew\u2011based (vegan) yogurt specifics\n1. Soaking: soak raw cashews 4+ hours or overnight. Rinse thoroughly.\n2. Blending: blend very smooth using a high-speed blender. Start with a low water ratio (e.g., 1 cup cashews : 1 cup water) and adjust\u2014use less water for thicker cream.\n3. Pasteurize/heat step: for safety and better texture you can gently heat the cashew cream to ~180\u00b0F, then cool to ~110\u2013115\u00b0F before adding starter. (Some recipes skip heating and rely on clean technique, but heating helps consistency and safety.)\n4. Thickening: cashew yogurt often needs a thickener to set well. Options:\n   - Agar-agar dissolved in hot liquid (vegan setting agent).\n   - Tapioca starch or arrowroot slurry (added at end and heated briefly).\n   - Small amount of coconut cream or a bit of xanthan gum for mouthfeel (use sparingly).\n5. Cultures: use a vegan yogurt starter or probiotic capsules (ensure they contain live strains suitable for yogurt). Add only after the base is cooled to ~110\u2013115\u00b0F.\n6. Incubation: keep warm ~100\u2013115\u00b0F for 8\u201312 hours. Cashew yogurt may not set as firmly as dairy; chilling overnight helps. If too thin, try less water, add agar/tapioca, or strain (though straining will reduce volume).\n7. Straining: a nut\u2011milk bag or fine mesh + cheesecloth works. Nut\u2011milk bag is easiest and less messy.\n\nF. Practical checklist to follow on your next cook\n1. Prep & brown: brown proteins and saut\u00e9 aromatics; deglaze pan.\n2. Layer veg appropriately (root veg bottom).\n3. Use correct liquid amount; remember slow cooker doesn\u2019t evaporate much.\n4. Use thermometer for yogurt and for learning your cooker\u2019s hot/low behavior.\n5. Add delicate items (fresh herbs, dairy, soft veg) near the end.\n6. Finish & adjust: thicken, add salt/acid, fresh herbs, and taste before serving.\n7. Make notes: time, heat setting, cut sizes\u2014this builds a personal recipe bank for consistent results.\n\nG. Quick recipe ideas with one-line slow\u2011cooker tips\n- Lentil curry: saut\u00e9 spices first; use red lentils for faster thickening.\n- Black bean chili: use canned beans or pre\u2011soaked beans; add tomatoes after beans are softened.\n- Chickpea & spinach curry: add spinach in last 20 minutes; stir in coconut milk at end.\n- Vegan \u201cpulled\u201d jackfruit: drain canned jackfruit, saut\u00e9 briefly, then slow cook in sauce; add BBQ at end.\n\nIf you want, pick one problem you\u2019re seeing most often (thin sauces, mushy veg, yogurt not setting, bland flavor, etc.) and I\u2019ll give a focused troubleshooting sequence and one tested recipe tailored to your slow cooker.", "autoeval_label": {"model": "gpt-4o-2024-08-06", "label": true}}
{"question_id": "06f04340", "hypothesis": "Step 1 \u2014 extract relevant info from your chats\n- You grow and cook with fresh basil and mint (you\u2019ve used both a lot).\n- You\u2019ve harvested cherry tomatoes from your garden.\n- You have (or grow) pepper plants (you talked about treating aphids on peppers).\n- You prefer relatively healthy, fresh, Mediterranean-style meals (you\u2019ve mentioned grilled chicken/fish, quinoa salad, mixed greens, vinaigrette).\n- You like fruit-based desserts (you planned a lemon-blueberry poppy-seed cake for another meal).\n\nStep 2 \u2014 reason over the info to pick a dinner that uses your homegrown ingredients\n- Basil, mint and cherry tomatoes are bright, fresh herbs/produce that pair well with grilled proteins, pasta, salads, and chilled sauces.\n- Peppers give you a great vehicle for stuffing/roasting or grilling alongside a main.\n- You already have a successful quinoa + roasted veg + mixed greens vibe, so a menu built around fresh herb sauces, grilled protein (or vegetarian alternative), a tomato/basil starter, and a stuffed-roasted pepper side will highlight your homegrown ingredients and stay light and crowd-pleasing.\n- Make-ahead items (pesto/chimichurri/quinoa) let you enjoy the evening.\n\nStep 3 \u2014 menu suggestions (three options) with quick recipes & tips\n\nOption A \u2014 Grilled Lemon-Herb Chicken + Basil-Mint Chimichurri\n- Starter: Cherry tomato & basil bruschetta (toasted baguette, chopped cherry tomatoes, minced basil, garlic, olive oil, splash balsamic, salt & pepper).\n- Main: Grilled lemon-herb chicken (marinate breasts/thighs in lemon juice, olive oil, garlic, salt, pepper, a little chopped basil; grill).\n- Sauce: Basil-mint chimichurri \u2014 in a blender or food processor combine 1 cup basil leaves + 1/2 cup mint leaves + 1/4 cup parsley (optional) + 2 garlic cloves + 1/2 tsp red pepper flakes + 2 tbsp red wine vinegar + 1/3\u20131/2 cup olive oil + salt & pepper. Taste and adjust. Spoon over the sliced chicken.\n- Side: Quinoa salad with roasted vegetables and mixed greens (you already liked this \u2014 toss warm quinoa + roasted veg + mixed greens + a lemon vinaigrette; toss with extra chopped basil).\n- Drink: Basil-mint lemonade (or infused water).\n- Dessert: Lemon-blueberry glaze cake (you already planned this) or fresh berries with a dollop of Greek yogurt and mint.\n\nMake-ahead tips: make chimichurri and quinoa salad earlier in the day (chimichurri benefits from 1\u20132 hours rest); bruschetta assembled just before serving so bread stays crisp.\n\nOption B \u2014 Pan/Grilled Fish with Basil Pesto + Tomato-Mint Salad\n- Starter: Caprese skewers (cherry tomatoes + small mozzarella + basil leaves, drizzle olive oil and balsamic).\n- Main: Grilled or pan-seared white fish or salmon topped with basil pesto (pesto: basil, garlic, pine nuts or almonds, Parmesan, olive oil, salt \u2014 blitz to desired texture).\n- Side 1: Warm roasted peppers (halved, tossed with olive oil, salt) or stuffed peppers: fill halved peppers with cooked quinoa, chopped cherry tomatoes, basil, feta, olive oil, bake 20\u201325 min.\n- Side 2: Tomato-mint salad \u2014 halved cherry tomatoes, thinly sliced red onion, chopped mint, olive oil, lemon juice, salt & pepper.\n- Drink: Sparkling water with mint sprigs.\n- Dessert: Yogurt parfait with granola and blueberries (use granola you made) \u2014 mint garnish.\n\nMake-ahead: pesto stores well refrigerated for a couple days or frozen in ice-cube trays; stuffed peppers can be assembled ahead and baked before serving.\n\nOption C \u2014 Vegetarian Pasta Night (fast, comforting)\n- Starter: Garlic-roasted cherry tomatoes (roast tomatoes with olive oil, garlic, salt until blistered) served with crusty bread.\n- Main: Lemony cherry-tomato & basil pasta \u2014 toss hot pasta with olive oil, garlic, lemon zest/juice, blistered cherry tomatoes, chopped basil, red pepper flakes, finish with Parmesan and a handful of toasted walnuts for crunch.\n- Side: Mixed greens with mint-lime vinaigrette (mixed greens, sliced cucumbers, crumbled goat or feta; dressing: lime juice, olive oil, chopped mint, salt).\n- Optional: Parmesan-topped grilled halloumi or grilled stuffed peppers for heft.\n- Dessert: Sliced fruit with honey and mint.\n\nStep 4 \u2014 quick shopping / prep checklist (if you want one)\n- Proteins: chicken breasts/thighs or fish fillets (or halloumi/feta for vegetarian).\n- Pantry: olive oil, red wine vinegar or lemon, garlic, pasta or quinoa, nuts (pine nuts/almonds/walnuts), Parmesan or other cheese, balsamic (optional).\n- Extras: baguette, mozzarella (for caprese), Greek yogurt/granola (if doing parfait), lemons.\n- From the garden: basil, mint, cherry tomatoes, peppers, mixed greens.\n\nStep 5 \u2014 timing plan for a relaxed weekend dinner\n- Morning / earlier day: Make pesto or chimichurri; cook quinoa and roast vegetables; bake stuffed peppers if using; chill drinks.\n- 30\u201345 minutes before guests: Start grill or preheat oven; marinate chicken or season fish.\n- 10\u201315 minutes before serving: Toast bread & assemble bruschetta or skewers; cook protein; warm or finish sides; toss salads last-minute.\n- Serve warm mains with fresh herb sauce on top and use extra herbs as garnish.\n\nIf you tell me which protein you prefer (chicken, fish, vegetarian) and roughly how many people you\u2019re serving, I can give exact ingredient amounts and a timed schedule. Which option appeals to you?", "autoeval_label": {"model": "gpt-4o-2024-08-06", "label": true}}
{"question_id": "6b7dfb22", "hypothesis": "Step 1 \u2014 extract relevant information from your previous chats\n- You paint regularly (interest in flowers, realism, texture).\n- You\u2019re using acrylics and palette knives / want to add more texture (asked about palette-knife techniques).\n- You set up (or are planning) a small apartment studio and care about optimizing your workspace.\n- You\u2019re doing a 30-day painting challenge and find inspiration from social media.\n- You sometimes get distracted or lose momentum mid-project and have used strategies (Pomodoro, breaking tasks down).\n- You enjoy making and upcycling crafts (e.g., denim projects) and photographing your work for online sale.\n- You have a small balcony herb garden and have been experimenting with other creative projects (cocktail infusions, DIY).\n- You like trying different materials and sweetener substitutions\u2014you\u2019re open to experimenting and tweaking recipes/processes.\n\nStep 2 \u2014 reason over that information and give concrete ways to find new inspiration\nBecause you already have routines, materials, texture interest, a garden, craft skills, and use social media for inspiration, the fastest way out of a rut is to combine those resources with targeted, small experiments and constraints that force new decisions. Below are grouped, actionable ideas (short exercises, medium-term projects, and habit changes) so you can pick what fits your energy right now.\n\nQuick exercises (10\u201360 minutes) to jolt creativity\n- 10-minute gesture flowers: set a timer for 10 minutes and do rapid studies of a single flower/leaf from your herb garden\u2014no detail, only shapes and values.  \n- Palette-knife only study: paint a 6\"x6\" piece using only a palette knife to explore texture and marks.  \n- 3-value challenge: paint a subject using only 3 tones (dark, mid, light) to rethink form and contrast.  \n- Color swap: take a reference photo and repaint it with an unlikely palette (e.g., cyan/pink/orange) to discover new color relationships.  \n- Non-dominant hand study: paint a small piece with your non-dominant hand for looseness and surprising marks.\n\nMedium projects (several sessions)\n- Herb series: paint a small series (4\u20136 paintings) of herbs from your balcony\u2014each piece focuses on a different element (close-up texture, silhouette, herb in a glass, herb in low light, herb-inspired abstraction).  \n- Material mash-up: incorporate nontraditional materials you enjoy (denim scraps, ribbon, embroidery) into a canvas for mixed-media texture.  \n- Theme swap: pick a non-art hobby (cocktails, upcycling) and interpret it visually\u2014paint a \u201cRosemary Gin Fizz\u201d still life, or a sequence showing a cocktail\u2019s ingredients.  \n- Scale switch: make one piece much larger or much smaller than your usual size\u2014scale changes perception and decision-making.\n\nWays to refresh technique and process\n- Limit tools: for a week, use only 2 brushes + palette knife OR only one tube of paint plus white\u2014constraints spark creativity.  \n- Teach a micro-class: preparing a short demo or tutorial (even just for a friend) forces you to articulate and rethink your approach.  \n- Try a different medium for a study (watercolor, gouache, oil pastel), then reinterpret the strongest result back in acrylics.  \n- Texture experiments: make small texture swatches mixing modeling paste, fabric, sand, gesso\u2014label each and note which you like for future pieces.\n\nInspiration sources beyond scrolling\n- Artist date: take a 1\u20132 hour \u201cartist date\u201d (Julia Cameron\u2019s idea)\u2014visit a botanical garden, flea market, or museum and sketch/photograph without pressure.  \n- A book or podcast binge: \u201cSteal Like an Artist\u201d (Austin Kleon) or podcasts interviewing painters for process insight.  \n- Local meetups / workshops: a short workshop can expose you to new methods and new peers.  \n- Photo-edit practice: photograph your subject, then play with color/contrast/filters\u2014use the edited photo as a new reference.\n\nCreative prompts to restart momentum\n- \u201cFrom life in 30\u201d: paint 30 small pieces from life over 30 days (you\u2019re already on a 30-day challenge\u2014set constraints like 20 min each).  \n- \u201cAltered reality\u201d: pick one flower and create 5 versions: botanical, realist, impressionist, abstract, and expressionist.  \n- \u201cTexture portrait\u201d: focus a whole painting on the texture (stamen center, leaf veins) rather than the whole flower.\n\nPractical habits and accountability\n- Micro-schedule: 25\u201345 minutes of focused painting (Pomodoro) 4\u20135 times a week\u2014short bursts beat infrequent marathons.  \n- Share progress publicly or with a friend weekly\u2014feedback and external deadlines help.  \n- Keep a visual idea notebook (photos, swatches, color notes, quick sketches) you can flip through when stuck.  \n- Rotate your workspace briefly (change lighting, reposition easel, hang a different reference) to change what you see.\n\nLow-pressure ways to test ideas\n- Make a \u201cfailure\u201d series: declare 5 pieces \u201cexperiments\u201d that aren\u2019t for sale\u2014freeing the pressure to take risks.  \n- Commission yourself a constraint: e.g., \u201cPaint a realistic flower using only palette knife marks,\u201d then evaluate what you learned.\n\nNext steps you can try tonight or this week\n1. Pick one quick exercise above (e.g., 10-minute gesture flower) and set a timer for tonight.  \n2. Pick one medium project (e.g., herb series) to plan across the next 2\u20133 weeks\u2014gather references and materials.  \n3. Add one habit: 3 Pomodoro painting sessions this week and post progress to a supportive art group or friend.\n\nIf you want, tell me: which of these ideas appeals to you most (palette-knife study, herb series, material mash-up, or something else)? I can give a tailored micro-plan you can start immediately.", "autoeval_label": {"mod