ng). Toasting amplifies the nut flavor and adds crunch that contrasts soft cookie centers.\n- Walnuts/pecans pair well with muscovado; sliced almonds + turbinado give a lighter crunchy finish.\n\n5) Salt and chocolate choices for contrast\n- Finish with flaky sea salt on hot cookies. The sweet + salt contrast makes flavors pop.\n- Use a mix of chocolate: chunks of 60\u201370% dark chocolate + some milk chocolate chips or chopped bittersweet chocolate for varied texture and flavor bursts.\n\n6) Subtle complexity: espresso, citrus, or spice\n- Add 1 tsp instant espresso powder to the dry mix to amplify chocolate without tasting \u201ccoffee.\u201d\n- Zest of half an orange or 1 tsp vanilla + 1/4 tsp almond extract complements muscovado and nuts.\n- A pinch of cinnamon, cardamom, or ginger can warm the profile\u2014use sparingly.\n\n7) Texture/tenderness tricks\n- For chewier centers: add an extra egg yolk (keeps them moist) and/or 1 tsp cornstarch.\n- For crisper edges: bake slightly hotter or flatten dough a bit before baking.\n- Always chill cookie dough at least 30\u201360 minutes (or up to 24\u201348 hours) \u2014 chilling improves flavor and controls spread.\n\nQuick recipe-style combo to try (single-batch adjustments)\n- Use your regular chocolate chip cookie recipe, then:\n  - Replace 1/3 of the brown/granulated sugar with muscovado (or add 3\u20134 tbsp muscovado).\n  - Brown the butter and cool to warm, then use in place of regular butter.\n  - Add 1 tsp instant espresso powder (optional).\n  - Fold in 1 cup chopped toasted walnuts (or 3/4 cup mixed walnuts + chopped dark chocolate chunks).\n  - Chill dough 2\u201324 hours.\n  - Scoop, top each ball with a few sliced almonds and a sprinkle of turbinado, bake as usual.\n  - Immediately after they come out, sprinkle with flaky sea salt.\n\nSmall experiments to run (one change at a time)\n- Batch A: your standard recipe + browned butter.\n- Batch B: same as A + 3 tbsp muscovado (replace part of sugar).\n- Batch 