ow cooker for the party; serve with sliced baguette, pita chips, and fresh veggies.\n2. Quick savory bites (choose one or two)\n   - Mini quiches or frittatas (make in muffin tins; vegetarian option available).\n   - Pull\u2011apart garlic & herb bread (baked ahead, reheat briefly).\n   - Roasted chickpeas (bake with olive oil + spices) for crunchy, protein\u2011rich snack.\n3. Desserts\n   - Lemon Lavender Pound Cake (your planned recipe with 1 tsp culinary dried lavender; make ahead\u2014day before). It\u2019s elegant, slices easily.\n   - Chewy Chocolate Chip Cookies (classic crowd pleaser; follow chewy tips: mix white + brown sugar, chill dough, remove while slightly soft).\n   - Optional: Brownies or blondie bites if you want a chocolate traybake in addition to cookies.\n4. Small snack bowls (no-bake)\n   - Homemade trail mix (nuts, seeds, dried fruit, a few dark chocolate chips).\n   - Flavored popcorn (olive oil base; try herby or smoked paprika).\n\nStep 4 \u2014 Quantities & timing guidance\n- Quantity (small gathering ~8\u201312 people):\n  - Dip: make ~3\u20134 cups (should serve 8\u201312 with other items).\n  - Cookies: 24\u201336 cookies (2\u20133 per person).\n  - Cake: one 9x5 loaf or 2-layer cake (12\u201314 slices).\n  - Mini quiches / bites: 24 pieces (2 each).\n  - Snack bowls: 2\u20133 cups each of trail mix / popcorn / roasted chickpeas.\n- Timeline:\n  - 2\u20133 days before: shop, make dough for cookies and freeze/chill; prep dry mix for trail mix.\n  - 1 day before: bake the cake and cookies; store cake wrapped (room temp up to 3 days) and cookies in airtight container.\n  - Day of morning: roast chickpeas; assemble mini quiches or reheat if made earlier; prepare dip ingredients and put in slow cooker to warm an hour before guests arrive (keep on warm).\n  - 30\u201360 minutes before: slice cake, lay out platters, warm bread briefly if needed.\n\nStep 5 \u2014 Serving, storage, and food\u2011safety tips\n- Keep the dip at a safe serving temperature in the slow cooker; don\u2019t let perishable items sit out more than 2 hours.\n- Label dishes with common allergens (nuts, dairy, gluten) for colleagues.\n- Cake storage: room temperature 2\u20133 days (well wrapped) or refrigerate up to 5 days; bring to room temp before serving for best flavor.\n- Cookie tips: store in airtight container; add a slice of bread to keep them soft.\n- Leftovers: cool within 2 hours, refrigerate, and reheat to 165\u00b0F (74\u00b0C) if reheating perishable items.\n\nStep 6 \u2014 Quick menu example you can use\n- Starter/snacks: flavored popcorn, roasted chickpeas, trail mix\n- Warm dip (slow cooker): Spinach & Artichoke Dip with sliced baguette & pita chips\n- Savory bites: mini quiches (mix of veggie and ham/cheese)\n- Desserts: Lemon Lavender Pound Cake + Chewy Chocolate Chip Cookies\n- Drinks: coffee/tea, sparkling water, a simple wine/beer option if appropriate\n\nIf you want, I can:\n- Provide the exact Spinach & Artichoke Dip slow\u2011cooker recipe using your mozzarella\u2013Parmesan blend and marinated artichokes.\n- Give the lemon lavender pound cake recipe with exact measurements and lavender handling tips.\n- Share the chewy cookie recipe with step\u2011by\u2011step tweaks for maximum chewiness.\n\nWhich of those would you like next?", "autoeval_label": {"model": "gpt-4o-2024-08-06", "label": true}}
{"question_id": "95228167", "hypothesis": "Step 1 \u2014 extract relevant information from your chat history\n- You\u2019re thinking about (or will be) visiting a music store this weekend to look at guitars.\n- You were comparing Fender Stratocaster vs Gibson Les Paul earlier and know the main differences (body/neck construction, scale length, pickups, tone, weight, playability).\n- You\u2019re interested in:\n  - Les Paul-style tone (warm, sustain, humbuckers) but also brighter/single-coil-like tones (you asked about coil\u2011tap / coil\u2011splitting).\n  - Playing in alternate tunings (specifically open D) and getting a good sound in that tuning.\n  - Using the guitar for a range of styles (rock, blues, possibly fingerpicking and more delicate lines).\n- You care about tonal flexibility, playability, and getting a guitar that suits both chunky electric tones and cleaner/acoustic-like textures.\n\nStep 2 \u2014 reasoned checklist and tips for what to look for in the store\n1. Decide what you want the guitar to do\n   - If you want heavier, warm, sustain-forward tones (rock/blues/metal) prioritize a Les Paul / humbucker platform.\n   - If you want a wider, brighter range and more single-coil character (clean, spanky sounds) try Strats or a Les Paul with coil\u2011split/tap to cover both worlds.\n\n2. Try the feel and playability first\n   - Neck profile and width: try several neck shapes. A comfortable neck is more important than specs on paper.\n   - Scale length: 25.5\" (Strat) feels snappier and with more string tension; 24.75\" (Les Paul) feels warmer and easier for bending.\n   - Action and frets: check for fret buzz, low action that\u2019s still clean, and comfortable string height.\n   - Weight and balance: Les Pauls can be heavy. Make sure the weight is tolerable and the guitar balances on your strap.\n\n3. Test tone across pickup positions and settings\n   - Play through the store amp(s) you\u2019ll use (clean and overdriven settings). Try your usual amp or a store amp with similar voicing.\n   - On a Les Paul or humbucker guitar:\n     - Neck pickup for warm, rounded tones and fingerpicking.\n     - Bridge pickup for punch and riffing.\n     - If the guitar has coil\u2011split/tap, try the split position and listen for single\u2011coil clarity and presence.\n   - Play in open D (tune the guitar in-store) and:\n     - Strum open chords to hear resonance and balance.\n     - Fingerpick patterns (like the style you asked about) to check note clarity and string-to-string definition.\n   - Try different gain levels to see how the guitar behaves clean vs pushed.\n\n4. Check electronics and controls\n   - Work the volume and tone pots, toggle switches, and pickup coilsplits \u2014 listen for crackle or intermittent connection.\n   - If coil\u2011split/tap is important, clarify whether it\u2019s a true coil\u2011split (grounds one coil of a humbucker) or a coil\u2011tap (accesses a portion of the coil) \u2014 they sound different. Test both single-coil-like and full-humbucker modes.\n   - Listen for hum or noise in single-coil-like modes; decide if that\u2019s acceptable or if you\u2019d prefer noiseless options.\n\n5. Setup for alternate tunings (open D)\n   - Lower tunings can reveal intonation and nut/bridge issues. Tune to open D and check:\n     - Intonation accuracy up the neck.\n     - Whether the nut slots allow stable tuning (no binding).\n     - That the guitar rings and sustains evenly in open strings.\n   - Ask about string gauge \u2014 heavier gauges (e.g., .012\u2013.052 or .013\u2013.056) are often recommended for open D to keep tension and clarity.\n\n6. Hardware, build quality and maintenance factors\n   - Check the bridge (stability, adjustability), tuners (smooth, stable), and nut (material and slot quality).\n   - Look at finish and fretwork (no sharp fret ends).\n   - If buying used, inspect for repairs, cracks, or modifications and ask about service history.\n\n7. Practicals: new vs used, price, warranty and resale\n   - Try similar models at several price points to learn what differences matter to you.\n   - New guitars give warranty and setup; used can be good value but may need a setup.\n   - Ask about return policy, setup included, and whether the store will do a setup in your preferred tuning.\n\n8. Accessories and final checks\n   - Try the guitar with the strings you plan to use (or ask the store to fit heavier strings for open D).\n   - Bring or request a strap so you can stand and play; posture can change the feel.\n   - If you use pedals (compressor, OD, amp sim), bring them or request the store use them for testing coil\u2011split and gain tones.\n   - Check case or gig bag quality if included.\n\nA quick in\u2011store test routine to follow\n- Put the guitar on, get comfortable.\n- Tune to standard, play clean and driven through the store amp.\n- Tune to open D, play open chords, fingerpick, and single\u2011note lines.\n- Cycle pickups: bridge \u2192 middle/coil\u2011split \u2192 neck. Toggle coil\u2011split/tap and listen.\n- Check sustain, note clarity, and how the instrument responds to dynamics.\n- Inspect hardware, electronics, frets, and neck relief (press and bend notes for buzz).\n- Ask the staff about setups and string recommendations for open D.\n\nFinal note\nIf you like the Les Paul aesthetic and tone but want single\u2011coil-like clarity, specifically look for models with good coil\u2011split wiring (or plan a professional mod). If fingerpicking clarity in open D is a high priority, test several guitars (Les Pauls, Strats, and semi\u2011hollows) \u2014 some players find semi\u2011hollows or a Les Paul with great setup and lighter strings gives the best balance.\n\nHave fun at the shop \u2014 take time, try lots of guitars, and play what feels and sounds best to you in the tunings and styles you actually use. If you want, tell me what models you try and I can help you compare them.", "autoeval_label": {"model": "gpt-4o-2024-08-06", "labe